kalah for OS-9/68000====================ORIG is the package as the Microware OS-9 archive carried it for the 6809(c09_Kalah): Michael J. Knudsen's Kalah and Pigeon Plague, its manual,and move.a, his hand-optimised 6809 assembly for count() and makel().The archive's CMDS/kalah is a 6809 module and does not run here.  Thefiles beside ORIG build the OS-9 program.What changed, and why:  movesrc.c  count() and makel() in C, which move.a hand-optimises.  The             file says "DO NOT COMPILE, as MAKE.A is hand-optimized.  This             file is for reference only", but move.a is 6809 code, and a             68000 build needs the C.  It is built unchanged.  kalah.c    Its flag named abort collides with the C library's abort(),             so under OSK it is kalah_abort.             main() made standard output unbuffered.  Under OSK printf()             prints nothing to an unbuffered stdout (measured: fputs(),             putc() and write() do, printf() and fprintf() do not), so the             call is left out; a terminal still shows each prompt before             the answer is read.  show.c     count() returns a char and show.c called it undeclared, so             the 68000 took the whole register as an int: the Pigeons             totals read 661522 where the board held 18.  Under OSK it is             declared.eval.c builds unchanged.Measured: a Kalah game at skill 2 takes a first move, the computer answers("My Move (worth $1) is: 3"), -2 gives advice ("I suggest Bin #4"), and -1twice abandons the game; a Pigeons game shows both sides' totals, 18 atthe start.DOC/kalah/kalah.doc is the manual.Build: cc kalah.c eval.c show.c movesrc.c -DOSK